Alembic — database migrations
=============================

Run every command from `backend/` (script_location is relative):

    cd backend

The target database is resolved from `DATABASE_URL` (env / backend/.env) by
`env.py`, falling back to the value in `alembic.ini`. So the same migrations run
against SQLite locally and PostgreSQL in deployment without editing any file:

    # local (SQLite, uses the app's default DATABASE_URL)
    python -m alembic upgrade head

    # deployment (PostgreSQL)
    DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@host/db python -m alembic upgrade head

Common commands
---------------
    python -m alembic current            # revision the DB is at
    python -m alembic heads              # latest revision(s) in versions/
    python -m alembic history            # full revision graph
    python -m alembic check              # fail if models drift from migrations
    python -m alembic revision --autogenerate -m "add X"   # new migration
    python -m alembic upgrade head       # apply
    python -m alembic downgrade -1       # roll back one

Existing databases (created by create_all)
------------------------------------------
The app still creates the schema via `Base.metadata.create_all` on startup, so a
database that predates Alembic already has every table. Mark it as up to date so
`upgrade head` does not try to recreate them:

    python -m alembic stamp head

New databases (e.g. a fresh PostgreSQL in the cloud) should instead run
`upgrade head` to build the schema from the baseline migration.

Notes
-----
- `env.py` imports every model so autogenerate sees the full metadata. When you
  add a new model, add its import there too (same list as main.py's lifespan).
- SQLite migrations use batch mode (`render_as_batch`) because SQLite cannot
  ALTER most columns in place. No effect on PostgreSQL.
